\description{
Fit each run's map from retention time in minutes onto a spectral library's iRT scale from anchors you choose, and convert the times you send with them, in either direction.

Answers \strong{where should this library precursor elute in my run?} and \strong{what is this run's minute
on the library's scale?} A spectral library stores retention time on a run-independent scale,
iRT (Escher et al. 2012); a run reports minutes. mzLib's \code{IrtCalibration.Fit} maps one onto the
other from \strong{anchors}: precursors you identified confidently in the run, each with its observed
apex time in minutes and its library iRT. The fit is robust: it starts from binned medians and
downweights anchors far from the curve (Tukey bisquare), so some wrong identifications do not
bend it. It is strictly increasing, so it inverts. By default it is a LOWESS curve (Cleveland
1979) that follows a gradient that bends; \code{kind = "linear"} fits one straight line.
}
\details{
Each \strong{group} (a run, typically) is fitted on its own anchors, and the \strong{queries} you send for
it are converted with that fit: give a time in minutes to get its iRT, or an iRT to get the
minutes at which that precursor should elute in this run.

\strong{You choose the anchors.} mzLib applies no q-value cutoff, and mzLibR adds none. \strong{Send the
queries in the same call as their anchors}: mzLib keeps the fitted curve's points private, so a
calibration cannot be returned and used later. Refitting the same anchors gives the same map,
because mzLib sorts them first.
}
\value{
An \code{mzlibr_rt_calibration}.

  \code{records} is a data.frame with one row per anchor, then one per query, in the order sent:
  \code{group}, as sent; \code{role}, \code{"anchor"}, \code{"to_irt"} (you gave minutes) or \code{"to_minutes"} (you gave
  iRT); \code{rt_minutes} (min), as given, or the converted time for a \code{"to_minutes"} query, \code{NA} when
  the group was not fitted and you gave none; \code{irt} (iRT), as given, or the converted value for a
  \code{"to_irt"} query, \code{NA} likewise; \code{fitted_irt} (iRT), anchors only, the curve's iRT at the
  anchor's time, so \code{irt - fitted_irt} is its residual, \code{NA} on a query or when the group was not
  fitted; and \code{is_extrapolated}, whether \code{rt_minutes} lies outside the group's anchors, where the
  map is a straight-line continuation rather than a fit, \code{NA} when the group was not fitted.

  \code{groups} is a data.frame, one row per group in order of first appearance: \code{group}; \code{kind}, the
  shape mzLib fitted (\code{NA}: not fitted); \code{anchor_count}, the anchors it was fitted from (anchors;
  the anchors sent, if refused); \code{query_count}, the times converted (queries); \code{residual_sd}, how
  far the anchors that fit sit from the curve, in \strong{iRT units}, mzLib's robust residual SD with
  wrong anchors set aside (divide by the curve's slope, iRT per minute, to read it in minutes;
