\section{The traps}{


\strong{iRT is the scale of one library.} A calibration maps one run onto the scale of the library the
anchors' iRT values came from, so iRT from two libraries is not comparable. **\code{residual_sd} is
robust**: it understates the spread when many anchors are wrong, and a LOWESS fit to few anchors
can pass through nearly all of them, so a \code{residual_sd} near 0 there means an overfit, not a
perfect run. With few anchors, prefer \code{kind = "linear"}. \strong{Outside the first and last anchor the
map is a straight line}, not a fit: read \code{is_extrapolated} before trusting a converted time
there.

}
