\section{Caveats}{
\itemize{
\item iRT is the scale of the library the anchors' iRT values came from. A calibration maps one run onto that library's scale, so iRT values from two libraries are not comparable, and the same run calibrated against two libraries gives two maps.
\item The anchors are the caller's choice: mzLib asks for the observed apex retention time, in minutes, of confidently identified precursors paired with their library iRT (IrtCalibration.cs:29-31). The bridge applies no q-value cutoff and removes no duplicate. The fit is robust to some wrong anchors (binned medians, then Tukey bisquare reweighting), not to a wrong choice of them.
\item residual_sd is mzLib's robust residual SD, in iRT units, of the anchors that fit: residuals beyond 3 robust SDs are set aside before it is computed (IrtCalibration.InlierSd). It understates the spread when many anchors are wrong, and a LOWESS fit to few anchors can pass through nearly all of them, so a residual_sd near 0 there means an overfit, not a perfect run. irt minus fitted_irt on the anchor rows shows every residual.
\item Outside each group's first and last anchor the map continues as a straight line, not a fit (IrtCalibrationModel.IsExtrapolated). Read is_extrapolated before trusting a converted time there.
\item The fitted curve itself does not cross the wire: mzLib keeps its knots private and its constructor internal (IrtCalibrationModel), so a calibration cannot be returned and reused. Send the queries in the same call as their anchors; refitting the same anchors gives the same map, because mzLib sorts them first.
}
}
\section{Performance}{
Every call starts one bridge process, which costs a .NET start-up before any work. 
